Maven notes
What is Maven?
Maven is a Java project build tool.
It helps Java projects:
- Download libraries
- Compile Java code
- Run tests
- Create a .jar file
- Run Spring Boot applications
Your project uses Maven because it contains:
pom.xml
What is pom.xml?
pom.xml is Maven’s project configuration file.
It contains:
Project name: hotstar-backend
Java version: 21
Dependencies: Spring Boot, MySQL, JPA, Security, JWT
Maven reads pom.xml and downloads required libraries automatically.
Why do we need Maven?
Without Maven, you would need to download every .jar file manually, add it to the project, and manage compatible versions.
Maven does this automatically.
pom.xml
   ↓
Maven downloads dependencies
   ↓
Maven compiles Java code
   ↓
Maven runs the Spring Boot app
Important Maven commands
.\mvnw.cmd clean
Deletes old build files in the target folder.
.\mvnw.cmd compile
Compiles Java files.
.\mvnw.cmd test
Runs test classes.
.\mvnw.cmd package
Creates a JAR file in the target folder.
.\mvnw.cmd clean install
Deletes old files, compiles, tests, creates the JAR, and installs it locally.
.\mvnw.cmd spring-boot:run
Runs your Spring Boot backend.
What is Maven Wrapper?
Your project has:
mvnw
mvnw.cmd
This is Maven Wrapper.
Use this on Windows:
.\mvnw.cmd spring-boot:run
It ensures the project uses the correct Maven version, even if Maven is not installed globally.
Maven vs Spring Boot
Maven       = builds, downloads, and runs the project
Spring Boot = framework used to create the backend/API
Maven starts Spring Boot with:
spring-boot:run
Maven vs IntelliJ Application run
Application configuration
→ IntelliJ compiles and runs HotstarApplication.java

Maven configuration
→ Maven reads pom.xml and runs spring-boot:run
Normally both work.
On your computer, IntelliJ’s Application configuration has an internal NPE, so Maven is working better because it bypasses IntelliJ’s failing build server.
Do all Java projects use Maven?
No. Common Java build tools are:
Maven   → pom.xml
Gradle  → build.gradle or build.gradle.kts
Ant     → build.xml
Use Maven when the project has pom.xml.
Do frontend projects use Maven?
Usually no.
Your React frontend uses:
package.json
npm install
npm run dev
Your backend uses:
pom.xml
.\mvnw.cmd spring-boot:run
When should I use clean install?
Use it when:
- You changed dependencies in pom.xml
- You get strange old-build errors
- You want to verify that the full project builds
For normal development, use:
.\mvnw.cmd spring-boot:run
